294,"def_epa_per_play":0.08021},{"week":15,"opponent":"GB","home":false,"pf":13.0,"pa":21.0,"off_snaps":82,"epa_per_play":-0.1185,"pass_rate":0.6707,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5455,"def_epa_per_play":-0.09998},{"week":16,"opponent":"KC","home":true,"pf":3.0,"pa":26.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":-0.2484,"pass_rate":0.6207,"pass_rate_neutral":0.3333,"def_epa_per_play":0.1937},{"week":17,"opponent":"MIN","home":false,"pf":21.0,"pa":19.0,"off_snaps":75,"epa_per_play":-0.02994,"pass_rate":0.5467,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5686,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1369}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2019,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6142,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"},{"season":2025,"team":"NO","distance":0.6213,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2023,"team":"BUF","distance":0.6383,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"PIT","distance":0.6421,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"BUF","distance":0.6425,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2024,"team":"ATL","distance":0.5726,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"WAS","distance":0.5994,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6181,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2025,"team":"TEN","distance":0.6399,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennard Wilson"},{"season":2019,"team":"DEN","distance":0.6504,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bears went 8-8 in 2019 and were outscored 298 to 280. The defense did most of the work, finishing 9th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 26th in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the 2nd fewest tight ends on the field and the 3rd highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Bears offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd fewest tight ends on the field and the 3rd highest no-huddle rate in the league. Mitchell Trubisky ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Allen Robinson, who drew 27% of the targets.","defense":"The Bears defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 1st, and its weakest was sack rate, where it ranked 27th. Khalil Mack was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Nick Williams, who had 6 sacks despite playing only 50% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the 3rd highest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Bears were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 82% of field goals, which was about average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 21% of the money on the books.","players":"Mitchell Trubisky played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,138 yards. Allen Robinson was the clear top target with 27% of the looks and 1,147 receiving yards. David Montgomery handled most of the work in the backfield with 71% of the carries. On defense, Khalil Mack led the team with 8.5 sacks and Kyle Fuller picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Matt Nagy called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2019 Chargers under Shane Steichen and the 2025 Saints under Kellen Moore. Their defense looked most like the 2024 Falcons and the 2018 Commanders.","trends":"The offense went from 14th in EPA per play in 2018 to 26th in 2019, a big step back. The defense moved from 1st in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 9th in 2019, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was no-huddle rate, which went from 4% to 14%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}